RPLE</text><text x="138" y="78.61" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="middle" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" letter-spacing=".4" fill="var(--fg)">R-P</text><rect x="32" y="342" width="34" height="10" fill="var(--fg)" opacity=".78"/><text x="73" y="351" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--muted)">primary</text><rect x="154" y="342" width="23" height="10" fill="var(--fg)" opacity=".78"/><text x="184" y="351" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--muted)">secondary</text><rect x="276" y="342" width="12" height="10" fill="var(--fg)" opacity=".78"/><text x="295" y="351" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--muted)">tertiary</text><text x="200" y="380" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--muted)">how far a sector reaches inward marks its tier</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.7rem;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted);">The artists&rsquo; wheel: three primaries (<b>red, yellow, blue</b>), three secondaries mixed from pairs of them (<b>orange, green, purple</b>), and six tertiaries between &mdash; <b>R-O</b> red-orange, <b>Y-O</b> yellow-orange, <b>Y-G</b> yellow-green, <b>B-G</b> blue-green, <b>B-P</b> blue-purple, <b>R-P</b> red-purple. RYB is a <b>pigment-mixing convention, not a colorimetric system</b>: it has no defined coordinates, no white point and no measurable primaries, so no hex value here is authoritative. Its mixing rules also disagree with screen colour &mdash; on this wheel blue and yellow make green and red&rsquo;s complement is green, whereas blue and yellow <em>light</em> add to white and red&rsquo;s complement on screen is cyan.</figcaption></figure>
